orhood of an individual is. The crowding distance is defined as a **Manhattan distance** measure in the objective space:

- A larger crowding distance indicates a sparser neighborhood and thus a stronger preference for preservation;
- For each objective, individuals within a front are sorted by their objective value, and the pairwise distances of neighboring individuals are accumulated;
- Extreme solutions at both ends (best and worst values for a given objective) are assigned an infinite crowding distance to ensure they are always preserved.

---

- All individuals are first subjected to **non-dominated sorting**, producing Front 1, Front 2, etc.;
   - Individuals are then added to the next generation in order of fronts, starting from Front 1, until the new population is about to exceed the maximum population size:
     - For the last partially selected front (e.g., Front \(k\)):
       - If including all individuals in Front \(k\) would exceed the population limit, only a subset is chosen:
         - The **crowding distance** is computed for each individual in this front;
         - Individuals are then selected in descending order of

stance** is computed for each individual in this front;
         - Individuals are then selected in descending order of crowding distance until the population limit is reached.
   - In this way, the next generation comprises individuals that are both **high-quality** (i.e., belonging to better Pareto fronts) and **well-distributed** (i.e., with large crowding distances).

